[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Full-Text Queries: Match vs Term in Elasticsearch"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building search applications with Elasticsearch, choosing the correct query type is critical for performance and relevance. The two most fundamental query types are the match query and the term query. Although both are used to search for documents, they behave fundamentally differently based on how Elasticsearch analyzes data."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Core Difference: Analyzed vs Exact Matching"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Match Query: This is a full-text query. It analyzes the input string, breaks it down into individual tokens, applies stemming or lowercase filters, and searches for those tokens in the inverted index."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Term Query: This is a strict, exact-value query. It does not analyze the search text. It looks for the exact term as it is stored in the inverted index, making it ideal for keywords, IDs, enums, and booleans."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up Test Data"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let us create an index named 'articles' with a standard text field and a keyword field to demonstrate the differences between match and term queries."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"PUT /articles
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "title": {
        "type": "text"
      },
      "status": {
        "type": "keyword"
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, let us index a document containing a multi-word title and a keyword status."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"POST /articles/_doc/1
{
  "title": "Elasticsearch Full-Text Search",
  "status": "published"
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the Match Query"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The match query is designed to handle human language. If you search for 'Elasticsearch Search' using a match query, Elasticsearch will analyze the input into ['elasticsearch', 'search'] and match the document because both tokens exist in the inverted index for the title field."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"GET /articles/_search
{
  "query": {
    "match": {
      "title": "Elasticsearch Search"
    }
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the Term Query"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The term query looks for the exact term. Because text fields are analyzed, the title 'Elasticsearch Full-Text Search' is tokenized into ['elasticsearch', 'full', 'text', 'search']. Searching for the exact phrase 'Elasticsearch Full-Text Search' using a term query on the text field will yield no results, because the exact string is not stored as a single token."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"GET /articles/_search
{
  "query": {
    "term": {
      "title": "Elasticsearch Full-Text Search"
    }
  }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"However, term queries are extremely effective when targeting 'keyword' fields or exact values like IDs and statuses."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"GET /articles/_search
{
  "query": {
    "term": {
      "status": "published"
    }
  }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Summary"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use Match queries for full-text fields (type: text) where linguistic analysis (stemming, lowercase, stopwords) is required."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use Term queries for exact-value fields (type: keyword, integer, date, boolean) where analysis would interfere with precision."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid using term queries on text fields unless you specifically target raw sub-fields (e.g., title.keyword)."}]
